From: Piotr Kubik <piotr.kubik@adtran.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:29:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: Keep the Multi-AP state of a link that reconfiguration adds

A Link Reconfiguration Request frame carries no Multi-AP element.
__check_assoc_ies() still ran check_multi_ap() for a link that the
request adds. That cleared the WLAN_STA_MULTI_AP flag, which the link
station took over from the MLD station, and on a backhaul-only BSS
refused the link with "Non-Multi-AP STA tries to associate with
backhaul-only BSS". A Multi-AP backhaul STA could therefore never add a
link to an existing association.

The Multi-AP state belongs to the MLD association, as the SSID does,
which the same function already skips for a reconfiguration. Skip the
Multi-AP check for a link added by reconfiguration as well.

---
--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
@@ -5727,9 +5727,15 @@ static int __check_assoc_ies(struct host
 	if (resp != WLAN_STATUS_SUCCESS)
 		goto out;
 
-	resp = check_multi_ap(hapd, sta, elems->multi_ap, elems->multi_ap_len);
-	if (resp != WLAN_STATUS_SUCCESS)
-		goto out;
+	/* A Link Reconfiguration Request frame has no Multi-AP element. A link
+	 * that it adds keeps the Multi-AP state of the MLD association, which
+	 * the link station took over with the station flags. */
+	if (type != LINK_PARSE_RECONF) {
+		resp = check_multi_ap(hapd, sta, elems->multi_ap,
+				      elems->multi_ap_len);
+		if (resp != WLAN_STATUS_SUCCESS)
+			goto out;
+	}
 
 	resp = copy_sta_ht_capab(hapd, sta, elems->ht_capabilities);
 	if (resp != WLAN_STATUS_SUCCESS)
